The Macedonian people go to the ballot box to a referendum on changing the country's name
Voters will vote on Sunday whether their country's name will be changed to the "Republic of North Macedonia."
Voting of the people is seeking to resolve a decades-long dispute with the neighboring country of Greece, one of which is called Macedonia.
Athens has long insisted that the country’s northern neighbor’s name of the country’s claim of its territory and has repeatedly protested Macedonia’s offer of membership in the European Union and NATO.
The Macedonian President George Ivanov opposes the referendum and has said he will not pay attention to the vote.
However, supporters of the referendum, including Prime Minister Zuran Zaeef, believe that the name change is a cost to join the European Union and NATO.
