In a referendum, the Macedonian people announce that they want to change the name of their country.
The Macedonian people took part in a referendum on Sunday to vote to change their country's name to the "Republic of North Macedonia", a move that would resolve a decades-long dispute with Greece, which has prevented the country from joining the European Union and NATO.
Greece, which has a province called Macedonia, claims that the name of its northern neighbour is a kind of claim on the country's territory and has vetoed Macedonia's entry into NATO and the European Union.
The two administrations reached an agreement in June on the proposed new name, but nationalist dissidents believe the change would undermine the ethnic identity of Macedonia’s Slavic majority.
President George Ivanov has said he will not vote in the referendum, and the referendum embargo has raised doubts about whether the number of voters would reach at least 50 percent needed to make the referendum credible.
The question written on the ballot is: “Do you agree with the acceptance of an agreement with Greece and membership in NATO and the European Union?”
Supporters of the name change, including Prime Minister Zarman Zaeef, believe the fee must be paid by Macedonia, one of the countries resulting from the collapse of Yugoslavia, in order to be able to enter the body of the European Union and NATO.
‘79-year-old Eliora Georgiyevska, who lives in Scupié, said: “I’m here today to vote for the future of my country, for the youth of Macedonia so they can live freely under the umbrella of the European Union, because all of our lives will become safer.”
Members of the parliament have adequately stated that they are complying with the outcome of the election, although this is not legally necessary.
A name change requires a two-thirds majority.
The Provincial Election Commission has stated that there have been no reports of violations of the laws until 1 p.m.
However, the number of participants in the referendum did not exceed 16 percent, while in the recent parliamentary elections in 2016, about sixty percent of registered voters participated.
Josie Thanoski, 62-year-old, one of the voters in Skopié, said: “I voted because of my kids, we’re in Europe.”
The Macedonian Prime Minister Zuran Zaev, his hus wife, Zurika and his son Daşeko cast their votes in Strumica, Macedonia on September ۳۰۱۲۰۱۸ to open the country's way of joining NATO and the European Union.
In front of the parliament in Skopië, 54-year-old Vladimir Kavardarkov prepares a small podium and puts seats in front of tents set up by people who boycott the referendum.
“We belong to NATO and the European Union, but we want to join them proudly, not through the back door.”
“We are a poor country, but we also have dignity.
“If they don’t want to accept us as Macedonia, we can go back to others like China and Russia and be part of an integrated European and Asian society.”
Prime Minister Zaev says NATO membership brings a lot of investment for a country whose unemployment rate exceeds more than 20 percent.
"I believe that the majority of the people are in favor of this because more than eighty percent of our citizens want the European Union and NATO," Zappef said.
He said the answer would be “yes, there will be a confirmation of our future.”
A survey published by the Macedonian Policy Research Institute last Monday found that between 30 and 43 percent of those eligible will take part in the referendum — this amount is below the quorum.
Another survey conducted by the Macedonian television network Telma shows that 57 percent of respondents plan to vote on Sunday.
Of these, seventy percent said they would vote yes.
For the referendum to be successful, a 50 percent quorum plus a vote is required.
The failure of the referendum is the first serious blow to the pro-Western government's policies that came to power in May last year.
