John Edward: Language skills are essential for citizens on the global arena
Scotland’s independent schools have a high level of education, and it continued in 2018 with excellent results in exams, which can only be reinforced by individual and collective successes in sports, art, music, and other social affairs of society.
With more than 30,000 students across Scotland, represented by the Council of Independent Schools (SCIS), these schools strive to provide the best services to students and their parents.
Independent schools are looking to prepare students for graduate studies and higher education, their elective jobs and their position as citizens on the global arena.
As an educational department that can design and implement a custom educational program, we see that modern languages continue to exist as a favorite and desired topic in schools.
Nelson Mandela once said, “If you talk to a man in a language that he understands, you’ll get into his mind.
“If you talk to him in his own language, your words will come into his heart.”
It’s an important point that we can’t rely only in English to build relations and trust with the people of other countries.
From the results of this year’s exam, we can see that foreign language lessons are at the top of the league table with the highest acceptance rate in independent schools.
A total of sixty-eight percent of the students who study in foreign languages earned an excellent A degree.
Data collected from ۷۴ SCIS member schools shows that 72 percent of students who study Mandarin Chinese earned an excellent A degree, as well as 72 percent of people who have studied in the German language, 69 percent of those who have studied French, and 63 percent of those who study in the Spanish.
These statistics show that independent schools in Scotland see foreign languages as a critical skill for children and adolescents that they will undoubtedly need in the future.
At present, foreign languages are taken as a lesson, in the same proportion of STEM (sports, technology, engineering, and mathematics) in independent school curriculum and elsewhere.
A survey conducted by the British Employment and Skilling Commission in 2014 found that among the needs of employers for employment, seventeen per cent was related to the lack of language skills in the collection.
Therefore, the importance of language skills in order to prepare young people for future professions becomes more and greater.
Due to future job opportunities that require language, it can be seen to what extent these skills are necessary in the global arena.
Regardless of the profession that a person chooses, if the second language is learned, it will have a real advantage in the future that a lifetime can use.
The ability to communicate directly with foreigners automatically puts a multilingual person ahead of competitors.
According to the YouGov survey of more than 4,000 adults in the United Kingdom and in 2013, seventy-five percent of people did not have the ability to speak a foreign language to have a conversation, and France is the only language whose percentage is two digits, i.e. 15 percent of people can speak the language.
That’s why it’s important to invest in language education for today’s children.
Knowing different languages, especially the language of developing countries, increases children’s chances of finding a suitable job.
Within Scotland, different schools teach different languages.
A number of schools focus on modern classical languages, while others teach languages that will be more important to the UK by 2020, such as Mandarin or Japanese Chinese.
It doesn't matter which language your child is interested in, in any case there are several languages to choose from in schools, and educational staff who are an expert in this field are also available.
Scotland’s independent schools are moving in the direction of providing a learning environment to prepare children and equip them with the skills needed to succeed in the future.
Today, in a global business environment, it is not possible to deny the vital importance of foreign languages in the country’s future, so it must be incorporated into education.
In fact, modern languages should be considered as ‘international communication skills’.
In independent schools, the right of choice, diversity, and excellence are still offered to Scottish children.
It must be done (in French)
John Edward is the Director of the Council of Independent Schools of Scotland
