North Korea Says "No Way" to Disarmament Without Restrusm
North Korea's foreign minister told the United Nations on Saturday that the continuation of the sanctions has made his country more distrust to the United States and, under such circumstances, he will not unilaterally surrender its nuclear weapons.
Ri Yong Ho told the global body’s annual General Assembly that North Korea has taken “significant measures to demonstrate its goodwill” last year, including halting nuclear and missile tests, destroying the nuclear testing site and committing to the non-development of nuclear weapons and technology.
"However, we didn't receive any proportional response from the United States," he said.
“Without confidence in the United States, there will be no certainty about our national security, and under such a situation, we will not act unilaterally to denuclearize ourselves.”
While Ri reiterates North Korea’s repeated grievances about Washington’s “stage” denuclearization approach under which North Korea will be rewarded by implementing every step, its statement is noteworthy because Pyongyang, contrary to the past, does not fully reject the possibility of unilateral denuclearization.
Ri refers to a joint statement issued by Kim Jong Un and Donald Trump in the first official meeting between the current U.S. president and the North Korean leader on June 12 in Singapore, in which Kim has pledged to "disinte the Korean Peninsula's nuclear denuclear weapons", and Trump has promised to ensure North Korea's security.
North Korea has sought to formally end Korea’s 53-1950 war, but the U.S. has said Pyongyang must first give up its nuclear weapons.
Washington has also resisted calls to reduce tough international sanctions against North Korea.
“The U.S. is insisting on a ‘first-to-disternuclear disarmament’ approach, and by increasing the pressure of sanctions, it intends to achieve its goal by using coercion and even opposes ‘the declaration of the end of the war.’
Some ignorant people think that sanctions can bring us to our knees, it's an illusion.
“But here’s the matter that the continuation of sanctions is increasing our mistrust.”
Ri made a no mention of the schedule of the second meeting between Kim and Trump, which the U.S. president made at the United Nations a few days earlier this week.
Instead, the minister referred to the three meetings between Kim and South Korean leader Moon Jae over the past five months, adding: "If the other side was on the issue of nuclear disarmament, instead of the United States, South Korea wouldn't have fallen into such a stalemate."
However, Mr. Ri’s tone speech was quite different from last year. Last year, he told the U.N. General Assembly last year that it was unavoidable to “Mr. President” Trump’s  �Misside Man’ who is committing.
This year at the U.N., Trump, who threatened to "extreminate North Korea" last year, praised him a lot because of Kim's courage to move toward disarmament, but said a lot of work still needs to be done and that sanctions should remain until North Korea's denuclearization.
Trump said on Wednesday that he had no time frame for it, stressing: “If it takes two years, three years or five months, it doesn’t matter.”
China and Russia said the U.N. Security Council should reward Pyongyang for the steps it has taken.
U.S. Secretary of State Mike Pompeo told the U.N. Security Council on Thursday: "The implementation of the Security Council's sanctions must continue vigorously and without shortcomings until we make sure that nuclear denuclearization has been carried out entirely, final and approved."
The Security Council has unanimously increased North Korea's sanctions since 2006 in an effort to block the financing of Pyongyang's nuclear and ballistic missile programs.
Pompeo met with Ri on the sidelines of the United Nations General Assembly, after which he said he would go to Pyongyang again next month to prepare the ground for a second summit of the two countries.
Pompeo has traveled to North Korea three times earlier this year, but his last trip didn’t go well.
He left Pyongyang in July when he said there had been some progress, but North Korea accused him of “gangster's demands after just a few hours.”
In a meeting with Moon this month, North Korea pledged to destroy a missile site as well as a nuclear complex if the U.S. adopts “professional measures.”
He said Kim had told him that by "proportimate measures" of security guarantees that Trump had committed in Singapore and said he planned to move towards normalization of relations with Washington.
Harvard students take a course on getting enough rest.
This year, a none lesson at Harvard University, in an effort to combat the expansion of Macho’s culture in the “Night of Survivors” who consume a lot of caffeine, wants all undergraduate students to sleep more.
One of the university professors found that most of the world’s best university students are unaware of the basics related to how to take care of themselves.
Charles Czeisler, a professor of sleep medicine at Harvard School of Medicine and an expert at Brigham and Women’s Hospital, has designed this course and believes that the lesson in the United States is the first.
He decided to set up a course after a speech about the effect of lack of sleep on learning.
She told Telegraph: “At the end, a girl came to me and said, ‘Why now and in my last year?
“No one has ever told me about the importance of sleep – which surprised me.”
This curriculum has been included in the curriculum for the first time this year and explains to students how good sleep habits contribute to academic and athletic performance, as well as improve public health.
Paul Barreira, a professor of psychiatry at the Harvard School of Medicine and the executive director of health services at the university, said the university had decided to add this lesson to the curriculum after realizing that students were seriously sleepless throughout the week.
This one-hour lesson includes a series of interactive activities.
In one section, there's a picture of a dormitory room where students click on coffee cups, curtains, sneakers, and books, and information about the effect of caffeine, light, and how athletic performance is affected by lack of sleep.
In another part, participants are how long-term sleep deprivation can increase the risk of heart attack, stroke, depression, and cancer.
Then a map of the university’s campus with interactive icons encourages participants to think about their daily habits.
Professor Czeisler added: “We know it won’t immediately change the student’s behavior.
“But we believe that they have the right to know – just as you have the right to know the effects of smoking on health.”
The culture of “visuality” continues to exist, he said, adding that modern technology and the increasing pressure on students show that lack of sleep is a growing problem, he said.
Ensurance of getting enough, quality-of-the-quality sleep should be the student’s ‘secret weapons’ to deal with stress, fatigue, and anxiety — even to avoid being overweight, as lack of sleep keeps the brain hungry, which makes a person constantly feel hungry.
Raymond So, a 19-year-old California student who studied chemical and physical biology and attended a professor's class last year in his first year at Harvard, helped Professor Czeiseler design.
He said the lesson had opened his eyes and inspired him to try to present it throughout the university’s campus.
He expressed hope and said that the next step is to ask all graduate students to take a similar educational program before joining a competitive institution.
Professor Czeisler recommended that students should set up a warning to determine their bedtime as well as when they wake up and be aware of the harmful “blue light” effects emitted by electronic device screens and LED lighting systems that can relieve your circadian rhythm and lead to difficulty sleeping.
