Yearbook: Inventor of Geiger Count
And now a page of our "Sunday Morning": September 30, 1882, 136 years ago on such a day and counting ... the day the future physicist Johannes Wilhelm "Hans" Geiger was born in Germany.
Gieger developed a way to identify and measure radioactivity, an invention that eventually led to the construction of a device known as the Geiger counter.
By portraying Geiger's counter in the film "Coronado Bells", starring Roy Rogers and Dale Evans, scientists who didn't seem to be cowboys, Geiger's counter, which until now was one of the main foundations of science, also became one of the main foundations of public culture:
Man: ‘What’s that?
Rogers: “Geager’s counter is used to track radioactive minerals such as uranium.
“When you put this phone on the meat, you can really hear the effects of the atoms emitted by radioactivity in minerals.”
Evans: ‘Really, he’s going to leave now!’
Hans Geiger died in 1945 just a few days before his 63rd birthday.
But the invention named after him is still alive.
New Cancer Vaccine Could Teach the Immune System to See ‘The Patient’ Cells
New Cancer Vaccine Could Teach the Immune System to See and Kill the Patient Cells
Vaccine trains the immune system to identify the patient's cells as part of treatment
This method involves extracting immune cells from the patient and changing them in the laboratory.
They can then “see” a protein that is common in many cancers and then re-injected.
The experimental vaccine showed promising results in patients with various cancers.
A woman was treated with this vaccine, which teaches the immune system to identify "patient" cells, and it was observed that her ovarian cancer disappeared for more than 18 months.
The procedure involves extracting immune cells from the patient, changing them in the laboratory so that they can ‘see’ the common protein in many cancers, called HER2, and then re-injecting these cells.
"Our results show that we have a very promising vaccine," said Professor Jay Berzofsky of the U.S. National Cancer Institute in Bethesda, Maryland.
Professor Berzofsky explained that HER2 “causes the development of several types of cancer,” including breast, ovarian, lung, and colon cancers.
A similar approach to removing immune cells from the patient’s body and “educating” them on how to target cancer cells has been successful in the treatment of a type of leukemia.
