Kit Harington, Game of Thrones, was criticised for toxic masculinity syndrome
Kit Harington is known for playing the role of Jon Snow, a skilled swordsman in the violent, fantasy and medieval series of HBO's Game of Thrones.
But the 31-year-old actor has been criticised for the cliché of Machu's hero, and it is said that portraying such roles will often make young boys feel that they need to be harsh and strict to be respected.
In an interview with Sunday Times Culture, Kit said he believes “there was a problem” and he was asked how to deal with toxic masculinity in the Age of MeToo.
Kit, who was recently married to his supporting star in the Game of Thrones series Rose Leslie, who is also 31, acknowledged that he “feelly feels powerful” about solving the problem.
"Right now, I'm fully empowered - where did we go wrong about masculinity?" she said.
“What did we teach men when they were growing, in terms of the problem we’re seeing now?”
Kit believes that television, thanks to its very masculine characters, may be somewhat responsible for increasing toxic masculinity.
He continued, ‘What’s inherent and what’s taught?
What’s being taught on television and on the streets that make young boys feel like they should have this particular aspect of being a man?
I think it's really one of the big questions in our period - how do we change that?
“It’s obvious that there’s something wrong for young men.”
