HIV/AIDS: China Reports 14% Increase in Identification of New Cases
China has announced a 14% mutation in the number of citizens living with HIV and AIDS.
Health officials say more than 8,20,000 people in the country are infected.
About 40,000 new cases were reported only in the second quarter of 2018.
In the vast majority of new cases, the disease has been transmitted through sex, indicating a change from the past.
Traditionally, HIV has spread rapidly in some parts of China due to contaminated blood transfusions.
Chinese health officials told a conference in Yunnan province, but the number of people who have been infected with HIV in this way has decreased and has reached almost zero.
However, the number of people living in China with HIV and AIDS has increased by 100,000 annually.
The transmission of HIV through sex is an acute problem in China’s LGBT community.
Homosexuality was de-ollegalized in China in 1997, but discrimination against LGBT people is very common.
Research estimates that due to the country’s conservative values, 90-70% of men who had sex with men will eventually marry women.
Many cases of transmission of the disease occur due to insufficient sexual protection in such relationships.
Since 2003, the Chinese government has promised to provide universal access to HIV drugs as part of efforts to tackle the problem.
Maxine Waters Rejects His Employee's Hand in Disclosure of GOP Senators
U.S. Representative Maxine Waters on Saturday rejected allegations that one of its employees had posted the personal information of three U.S. Republican senators on the Wikipedia pages of the lawmakers.
The Los Angeles Democrat said the allegations had been made by “Far-right Party” experts and websites.
In a statement posted on Twitter, Waters said: “It’s a shame, a lie again.”
According to reports, the published information included the home address and phone number of Senate senators Mr. Lindsey Graham of Southern California and Mike Lee and Orrin Hatch both from the state of Utah.
Information that was posted on the Internet on Thursday was posted by an unidentified person at Capitol Hill during a Senate hearing on allegations of sexual mistreatment that had been filed against the candidate of the Supreme Court of Brett Kavanaugh.
The revelation came shortly after the three senators questioned Kavanaugh.
Hill reported that conservative sites such as Gateway Pundit and RedState reported that the IP address, which identifies the source of the posts, was affiliated with the Office of Waters and released information from a Waters employee.
Waters continued, “This baseless claim is completely false and absolutely false.”
“My employee – whose identity, personal information and security have been jeopardized as a result of these false and fraudulent claims – were not responsible for exposing this information.
This baseless claim is completely false and absolutely false.”
The Water's statement quickly sparked online criticism, including from former White House Press Secretary Ari Fleischer.
Fleischer wrote, “This denial was accompanied by anger.”
“It shows that he doesn’t have the right spirit to join Congress.
When someone is accused of something he didn’t do, they shouldn’t get angry.
It shouldn’t be tough.
Don't question the motives of the accuser.
Rather, it must be calm and calm.”
Fleischer seemed to be comparing Water’s response to Democrats criticizing Judge Kavanaugh, who was accused by critics who appeared to be very angry at Thursday’s hearing.
Omar Navarro, one of the Republican candidates who planned to replace Waters in the mid-term elections, also voiced his opinion on Twitter.
He tweeted: "If it's true, it's a big claim."
In a statement, Waters said its office had alerted "the relevant authorities and legal institutions" against these fraudulent claims.
“We will make sure that the perpetrators of this matter are identified and take legal responsibility for all the destructive and dangerous actions they’ve taken against one person or all of my employees.”
