The Labour Party rejected the fact that it's designing a plan that allows the British to work four days a week, but to get paid five days.
Jeremy Corbyn's Labor Party is planning an extreme plan that will cause the British to work four days a week – but get paid for five days.
According to reports, the Party urges corporate presidents to give employees a day more paid leave by using savings created through the Artificial Intelligence (AI).
The plan will cause employees to enjoy the end-of-week holiday – and continue to receive the same salary.
Sources say the idea is “appropriate” with the party’s economic plan, and the party is trying to move the country towards workers’ interests.
The movement towards a four-day working week has been endorsed by the Congress of Trade Unions as a way for workers to benefit from a changing economy.
A senior Labour source told the Sunday Times: “It’s expected to re-examize policies before the end of the year.
“It won’t happen overnight, but the four-day-working week’s plan is a demand that is consistent with the party’s approach to balancing the economy in favor of the worker, as well as the party’s overall industrial strategy.
The Labour Party is not the first party to endorse such an idea, but in its 2017 election campaign, the Green Party pledged to implement the four working-by-day-week plan.
However, this demand is currently not generally approved by the Labour Party.
"There is no four working days a week of the party's politics, and the party has no plans for it."
In the shadow, John McDonnell used the Labour Party's conference last week to explain his opinion of a socialist revolution in the economy.
McDonnell said he had decided to take back power at public service companies from “unknown” and “user-to-use” executives.
Also, the shadow prime minister’s plans means that shareholders of water companies can’t take out all of their shares because the Labour Party government can desecrate ‘deduction’ based on the violations they’ve committed.
He also confirmed that he plans to bring workers into the board of directors of the companies and create group-owned funds to transfer 10 percent of the capital of private sector companies to the employees, which means they will reach up to £,500 annual dividends.
