What is the International Citizenship Festival and has it done something to reduce poverty?
This Saturday, New York will host the Global Citizenship Festival, which will be an annual music event where a very well-known group of stars performs and has a very important mission: an end to global poverty.
The Global Citizenship Festival, which is now in its seventh year, has brought tens of thousands of people to the Central Great Lawn Park to not only enjoy the performances of artists such as Janet Jackson, Cardi B, and Shawndes Mendes, but also to raise awareness about the main goal of the event, which is to end extreme poverty by 2030.
The Global Citizenship Festival, which began in 2012, is one of the developed sections of the Global Poverty Project, which is an international support group hoping to eliminate poverty by increasing the number of people active in the fight against poverty.
Those who want to participate in a concert, in order to receive a free ticket (unless they wish to pay for a VIP ticket), must help raise awareness about their goal of poverty by performing a set of tasks or “actions” such as volunteer work, emailing a global leader, calling, or any other effective way.
But how successful has universal citizenship been while having 12 years to achieve its goal so far?
Is the idea of rewarding people with a free concert the right way to encourage them to make a call to take action or another kind of so-called “clicktivism?” – people who have done a very big job by signing an online petition or posting a tweet.
Global citizenship says it has recorded more than 19 million “actions” on behalf of its supporters since 2011 who have worked hard to achieve a variety of goals.
They say these measures have helped global leaders to make a move and adopt commitments and policies at a cost of more than $37 billion so that they can have a positive impact on the lives of more than 2.25 billion by 2030.
In early 2018, the group pointed to 390 commitments and announcements that came from their actions, of which at least $10 billion had already been paid or collected in the form of donations.
The group estimates that guaranteed funds so far have had a direct impact on about 649 million people worldwide.
Some key commitments include The Power of Nutrition, a group of UK-based investors and performers who, after receiving more than 4,700 tweets from global citizenship, have pledged to “help children grow up and reach their full potential” and have pledged $35 million to help Rwanda eliminate malnutrition in the region.
"With the support of the UK government, donors, donors, national governments, and global citizens like you can eliminate malnutritition, which is a social injustice and link to history.
The group also said that after more than 5,000 measures taken to ask the UK to improve the nutrition of mothers and children, the government announced that it had allocated for a project called feeding power, which would include food intervention for 5 million women and children.
In response to one of the most frequently asked questions on his website: “What makes you think we can eliminate absolute poverty?”
“It’s going to be a long and difficult – sometimes we’re going to fall and we won’t succeed.
But like the civil rights and anti-apartheide movements that have been done before us, we will succeed because together we are more powerful.
Janet Jackson, the Weeknd, Shawn Mendes, Cardi B, Janelle Monáe are some of the artists who will perform at this year's New York event hosted by Deborra-Lee Furness and Hugh Jackman.
