World Wide Web Inventor Plans to Launch a New Internet to Compete with Google and Facebook
Tim Berners-Lee, the inventor of the World Wide Web, is launching a startup looking to compete with Facebook, Amazon and Google.
The latest project of this technology legend is Incrupt, a company that is based on the erners-Lee open-source platform, Solid.
Solid allows users to choose where their information to be stored and who is allowed to access.
Berners-Lee, in an exclusive interview with Fast Company, joked that the idea behind Incrupt is a “global monarchy.”
He said, ‘We need to do it right now.’
“It’s a historic moment.”
The program uses solid technology to allow people to create “online storage of their personal information” or POD.
This space can contain an audience list, work list, calendar, music library and other personal and professional tools.
It’s like Google Drive, Microsoft Outlook, Slack, and Spotify are all available simultaneously in the same browser.
What this online storage space makes personal information unique is that it depends entirely on the user who can access what kind of information.
The company calls “personal empowerment through information.”
According to John Bruce’s CEO, Inrupt’s idea is that the company can provide the right resources, processes, and skills to help put all-in-on-one solid.
The company now consists of Berners-Lee, Bruce, a security platform purchased by IBM, several developers that have been contracted to work on the project, and a series of volunteer programmers.
From this week, tech developers from around the world will be able to create their own decentralized applications using tools available on the Inrupt website.
Berners-Lee said he and his team weren’t talking “Facebook and Google about creating or failing to make a complete change that all their business models will turn them upside down overnight.
‘We don’t get their permission.”
Berners-Lee wrote in a post published on Saturday that “the Inrupt mission is to provide commercial energy and an ecosystem to help protect the integrity and quality of the new web that will be built on Solid.”
Berners-Lee revolutionized the Internet in 1994 when the consortium created the World Wide Web at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology.
Berners-Lee in recent months has been an influential face of network neutrality.
Berners-Lee will remain open even with the launch of Inrupt, the co-founder and consortium of the World Wide Web, the Web Foundation and the Information Institute.
Berners-Lee added: ‘I am incredibly optimistic about the next web course.
Varn Bernard: Pastor of the Victoria Creade in World War I
The only pastor of the Church of England who was able to receive the Victorian Cross as a warrior in World War I was honored on the 100th Anniversary of receiving the award in his hometown.
Pastor Lieutenant Colonel Bernard Vann received the award on September 29, 1918 in an attack on Bellenglise and Lehaucourt.
However, he was killed four days later by a sniper and never learned that he had received England’s highest military honor.
On Saturday, a commemorative stone was unveiled by his two grandchildren during a parade at Rushden, Northamptonshire.
One of his grandchildren, Michael Vann, said that exactly 100 years after his grandfather's masterpiece, which led to this honor, his commemorative stone was "religious and intelligent."
