He later attacked the sline and, with "full courage" alone and before the cannon attack, steered the line forward and killed three companies.
Lieutenant Colonel Vann was killed by a German sniper on October 4, 1918, just a month before the end of the war.
72-year-old Michael Vann said his grandfather’s actions “was something that I know I could never have been to be that good, but was considered small.”
He and his brother, Dr. James Vann, after the parade, which was led by the Bentwood Imperial Youth Group, laid a wreath on the ground.
"He feels very proud to have played a role in the parade," Vann said, adding that "guallery is represented by a real-supported hero done by so many people."
