The Four Baden-Wuerttemberg-class ships that the Navy ordered in 2007 will replace old Bremen-class warships.
It’s clear that these ships will benefit from a powerful cannon, a series of anti-aircraft and anti-ship missiles, as well as several stealth technologies such as the reduction of radar, infrared and audio effects.
Other important features include a longer storage period – it’s likely to be possible to employ new carriers for a maximum of two years away from their own beaches.
“Continuous delays nevertheless mean that these modern warships—are said to allow Germany to increase its power in overseas ‘will become old until it enters the navy.
Last year, when the German navy formally refused to operate the F125 and returned it to the Blohm & Voss shipbuilding plant in Hamburg.
