There’s very little information about the reasons for this return, but the German media have said there were a number of “software and hardware flaws” that used the ship if it was used for a war mission.
Software flaws are particularly important because Baden-Wurettemberg-class ships will be operated by 120 sailors, only halving the manpower of older Bremen-class carriers.
Also, it turned out that the ship is significantly heavier than the standard, which would reduce its performance, limiting the Navy’s ability to add future upgrades.
The 7,000-ton Rheinland-Pfalz is believed to be twice as heavier than similar-class ships used by Germany in World War II.
