The National Trust examines the hidden lives of bats
A new research is underway in part of Scottish Highlands aimed at determining how bat bats use the area to hunt food.
It is hoped that the findings of this research will reveal new angles of the behavior of these unique bird mammals and help guide their conservation activities in the future.
The study, conducted by scientists at the National Trust for Scotland, tracks short-leated bats and dwarf bats, as well as long-eyed bats and bats in Inverewe Gardens in Wester Ross.
Special registrators will be placed in key locations around the area to track bat activities throughout the season.
Employees and NHS volunteers will also conduct animated mapping using manual detectors.
Specialized voice analysis will determine all the recordings, the frequency of the bat sounds, as well as how each species will do.
Then a map and a report from their habitat will be created to prepare a detailed image of the area’s scale of their behavior.
Natural Conservation Advisor Rob Dewar for NTS expressed hope and said the results of the study would determine which habitat areas are more important to bats and how they will be used by each species.
This information will help determine the benefits of measures related to habitat management, including the creation of meadows and how to preserve forests in the best for bats and other related species.
Over the past century, the population of bats in Scotland and across England has declined dramatically.
They are threatened on behalf of construction and development work that affects their nests and destroys their habitats.
Wind turbines and lighting can also be dangerous for them, as well as insecticide paper and some chemical compounds of building materials, as well as domestic cat attacks.
Bats aren't actually blind.
However, due to the habit of night hunting, when it comes to prey, their ears are more useful than their eyes.
They use a complex evocation technique to pinpoint the exact location of insects and obstacles on their flight path.
NTS, which is responsible for taking care of more than 270 historical buildings, 38 important gardens and 76,000 hectares of land across the country, takes the issue of bats very seriously.
They have ten trained professionals who regularly perform mapping operations, checking nests and sometimes rescue operations.
The organization has even designated Threave's terrain in Dumfries and Galloway, a habitat of eight of Scotland's ten bat species, as the first and only protected area of bats in Scotland.
David Thompson’s regional manager says the area is an ideal territory for them.
"Here at Threave," he said. "Here at Threave, we've got a very good area for bats."
“We have old buildings, many old trees, and all suitable habitats.
“But there’s a lot about bats that are still unknown, so what we’re doing here and in other areas will help us find out what else they’ll need to grow.”
He stressed the importance of investigating the presence of bats before repairing operations in buildings, as the unintentional destruction of a mother bat may result in the killing of up to 400 female and young bats, which is likely to result in the loss of an entire local population.
Bats are under protection and killing, harassing and harassing them and destroying their nests is illegal.
Elisabeth Ferrell, the Scottish director of Bat Conservation Trust, urged people to help seriously.
“We still need to learn a lot about our bats, and for many of our species, we still don’t know how their populations will survive.”
