British Trade Minister Greg Clark has said that if the UK loses its position as a world leader in the automaker after Brexit, it will "evokes their usual regret."
“It’s ‘worrying’ that the UK’s Toyota unit told the BBC it would temporarily suspend production at Burnaston factory near Derby if the UK leaves the European Union without an agreement.
"We need an agreement," Mr. Clark said.
The Japanese automaker has said the impact of cross-border delays in the event of an unrequisted Brexit could cost jobs.
Burnaston, which manufactures Auris and Avensis vehicles, produced about 150,000 vehicles last year, 90% of which were exported to other EU countries.
"My view is that if the UK leaves the European Union at the end of March without an agreement, we're going to see a halt to production at our factory," said Marvin Cooke.
Other UK automakers, including Honda, BMW and Jaguar Land Rover, have also expressed concern about leaving the European Union without an agreement on how cross-border trade operates.
For e.g., BMW says that a month after Brexit, it will shut down its Mini production plant at Oxford.
The main concern about what automakers say is related to supply chain hazards in the event of a no-deal exit.
Toyota’s production line operates based on the “right timely” principle, so that parts from UK and EU suppliers reach the factory every 37 minutes to make the cars by order.
If the UK leaves the European Union without an agreement on March 29, it could be disrupted at the borders, which industries say could lead to delays and a shortage of parts.
Toyota said it would be impossible for us to keep our inventory at Derbyshire's plant for more than a day, so production will stop.
Mr Clarke said Ms Chequers Theresa May's plan for future relations with the European Union was “designed precisely to avoid these problems at the border.”
"We have to have an agreement. We want to have the best agreement to allow us not only to be successful right now," he told the BBC's Today Channel 4 program.
“An issue not only by Toyota but also by other manufacturers is that we should be able to fully continue using this successful supply chain.”
Toyota was unable to say how long the production would be halted, but warned that in the long run these additional costs could reduce factory competitiveness and eventually lead to job loss.
Peter Tsouvallaris, who has been working at Burnaston for 24 years and is in charge of calling the Unitee Union, said the concerns of union members are growing: “Based on my experience when these jobs are lost, they will never be revived again.
