U.S.-Chinese Trade War May Be in Beijing's Interest
Experts say gunshots to start a trade war between the U.S. and China were scarving, and as it is still too far to the end of the fight, a dispute between the two countries could benefit Beijing in the long term.
Earlier this year, U.S. President Donald Trump made the first warning by imposing taxes on Chinese export goods such as solar panels, steel and aluminum.
Trade tensions between the two countries culminated this week with new tariffs on €$150 billion (€150 billion) of goods, on half of all items imported from China to the United States.
In return, Beijing has retaliated in some way each time, the latter of which is the imposition of tariffs of ‘five to £10 per cent over €60 billion in U.S. goods.
China has sworn to compete with the U.S. step-by-step, and the world’s second-largest economy is unlikely to tremble so soon.
Getting out of Washington’s decision would mean surrender to his demands, but it’s very embarrassing for Chinese President Xi Jinping to slove publicly against the U.S.
However, experts say that if Beijing can play properly with its cards, U.S. trade war pressures could have a positive impact on China in the long run by reducing dependence between the two countries.
"The fact that the unthought-out political decision by Washington or Beijing could create a situation that will cause an economic collapse in any country is much more dangerous than what observers have already acknowledged," said Abigil Grace, a research fellow working on Asian affairs at the New American Center for Economic Security.
Syrian Foreign Minister Says Syria ‘Prepared to ‘Return ‘Reprieve of Refugees
Syria says it is ready for the voluntary return of refugees and is asking other countries to help rebuild it, which has destroyed more than seven years of war.
When Syrian Foreign Minister Walid Moallem spoke at the United Nations General Assembly, he said his country’s conditions were getting better.
"Today, thanks to the progress made in the fight against terrorism, the situation in Syria has become more stable and secure," he said.
The government is trying to rebuild the areas that the terrorists have destroyed and restore normal life to the country.
All conditions are ready for the voluntary return of refugees to the country, which they have left for the sake of terrorism and because of the unilateral economic measures that have affected their daily lives and their livelihoods.
According to the United Nations, more than 5.5 million Syrians have fled the country with the start of the war since 2011.
Another six-million people who still live in the country need humanitarian assistance.
"The Syrian regime welcomes assistance to rebuild the devastated country," Moallem said.
However, he stressed that he did not want conditional assistance as well as the help of countries that supported the Syrian unrest.
